{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c619","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0afcb-4eda-7472-95f4-397cf45aa943"},"sequence":359,"recorded_at":1780531400000713,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"de9112ff-c6c5-4929-8606-bedba1c8b82d","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"de9112ff-c6c5-4929-8606-bedba1c8b82d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"de9112ff-c6c5-4929-8606-bedba1c8b82d"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0afcc-6c35-72a2-b80f-a0ea74bcd5ee"},"task_id":"01a0afcc-6c35-72a2-b80f-a0ea74bcd5ee","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0afcc-6c35-72a2-b80f-a0ea74bcd5ee","chunk":"Read text file `README.md`.\n(3158 line(s) above)\n3159|with the negative `TestDecodeRefusesNonConptyBackendOnNativeWindows` legacy\n3160|refusal arm; and Section 2.4 at 4/4, bound to\n3161|[`internal/sessprofile`](internal/sessprofile), whose derivation, checkpoint\n3162|closure, fork projection, and mapping-failure clauses are discharged by the\n3163|profile derivation, heads, fork-pair, and mapping-resolution acceptance\n3164|cases), six are\n3165|`partial` (Section 13.13 at 9/11, bound to\n3166|[`internal/matjournal`](internal/matjournal) with the\n3167|[`internal/crashgate`](internal/crashgate) conformance harness, whose\n3168|undischarged clauses `13.13#4` and `13.13#5` are the rollback/parked\n3169|visibility sentences through event, CLI/status, audit, and\n3170|lifecycle-projection surfaces this repository has no binary or projection to\n3171|produce; Section 14.2 at 8/9, bound to\n3172|[`internal/cliresult`](internal/cliresult), whose undischarged clause `14.2#6`\n3173|is the process exit status this repository has no binary to produce; Section\n3174|5.3 at 7/8, bound to\n3175|[`internal/sessrepo`](internal/sessrepo), whose undischarged clause `5.3#5`\n3176|is the initiator-union maximum no takeover flow computes yet; Section\n3177|15.1 at 5/7 and Section 15.3 at 2/3, both bound to\n3178|[`internal/axerror`](internal/axerror); the three undischarged clauses there are\n3179|the RPC hello obligation `15.1#5`, the bootstrap-row sentence `15.1#6` that\n3180|binds the provider plugin rather than the host, and the hello-key and\n3181|TerminalBackend-capability prohibition `15.3#3` - this repository builds no\n3182|provider plugin, and advertises no Structured Error as a TerminalBackend\n3183|capability. Structural RPC hello frames are built by\n3184|[`internal/rpcwire`](internal/rpcwire), which rejects an error map key, and\n3185|consumed by the authenticated [`internal/hostchannel`](internal/hostchannel)\n3186|handshake, which frames failures only as Error envelopes and never as hello\n3187|keys; neither clause is enumerated against an acceptance case, so both stay\n3188|undischarged. The closed 16-capability admission registry in\n3189|[`internal/terminalbackend`](internal/terminalbackend) gates which operations\n3190|an admitted probe may confer; it is never advertised on an RPC hello path and\n3191|carries no Structured Error code, so the prohibition holds and the clause\n3192|stays undischarged. The same package carries the Section 4 lifecycle,\n3193|attach, entrypoint, replication, and historical-translation conformance\n3194|harness (`conformance.go`, exercised by `conformance_test.go`) and an\n3195|AST-derived refusal-arm inventory (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`) that\n3196|requires every production refusal arm to be declared with a resolving named\n3197|asserting test in both directions; behavioral proof stays with each row's\n3198|named test, which the inventory resolves textually; and Section 7.7 at 3/4,\n3199|bound to [`internal/provhost`](internal/provhost), whose undischarged clause\n3200|`7.7#2` is the machine-local-alias sentence - the omit rule is enforced, but a\n3201|shell word that expands to unrestricted mode without spelling a table token is\n3202|beyond argv inspection),\n3203|four are\n3204|`sliver` (Section 10.3, whose chunk offset invariant is\n3205|enforced by `validateBlobDescriptor` while its two receiver clauses have no\n3206|implementation; Section 5.5 at 1/3, whose discharged negative-battery clause\n3207|is enforced by identity creation and checking while the opaque-map content\n3208|rule stays half-decided; Section 8 at 4/12, whose discharged probe,\n3209|label-integrity, and row-separation clauses are enforced by the resume tuple\n3210|gate and the native-resume smoke while every materialization rule stays\n3211|unimplemented; and Section 2.2 at 4/22, bound to\n3212|[`internal/fencing`](internal/fencing), whose discharged clauses are the\n3213|single-owner, replica-restraint, winning-epoch-carriage, and\n3214|losing-event-rejection invariants while the replication, secret, store,\n3215|and directory invariants have no implementation), four are `unmeasured` (Sections 7.3, 13.12, 13.14.5 and 15.2, each of\n3216|which carries a gap saying why the scanner measures zero and what is missing),\n3217|and forty-four are `unevidenced`. Eleven sections are recorded unowned.\n3218|All 13 sections added by v0.6.0 name pending task owners in the reviewed\n3219|registry gaps; these assignments grant no runtime admission. The\n3220|[adoption ownership map](internal/traceability/adoption-v0.6.0.md) separates\n3221|shared runtime APIs, their callers, product conformance and upstream source\n3222|publication validators, and preserves historical acceptance obligations.\n3223|Assigned-scope admission therefore succeeds today for `-section 6.2` and\n3224|`-section 2.4` and nothing else; every other assignment is refused with its\n3225|ratio and its gap.\n3226|A `partial` binding is refused by assigned-scope admission exactly like an\n3227|`unevidenced` one: admission requires `full`.\n3228|\n3229|Two admitted bindings out of sixty cover five clauses, and that is\n3230|disclosed here rather than hidden: without Section 6.2 the admit path would only\n3231|ever be exercised synthetically. Its discharge is no longer positive-only: the\n3232|native-Windows lanes carry the positive arm and\n3233|`TestDecodeRefusesNonConptyBackendOnNativeWindows` the legacy refusal arm,\n3234|both registered against the `config-versioned-readers` acceptance case.\n3235|Section 2.4 is the first multi-clause admission: its four clauses are\n3236|discharged by the `sessprofile-derive`, `sessprofile-derive-heads`,\n3237|`sessprofile-fork-pair`, and `provhost-mapping-resolution` acceptance cases.\n3238|\n3239|That is a disclosure of the shipped state, not a target that was met.\n3240|`TestRunRefusesEveryAssignedSectionThatOnlySlivers` and\n3241|`TestVerifyAssignedSectionsRefusesEveryBindingThatOnlySlivers` pin every failing\n3242|binding with its exact measured ratio, so a section that becomes covered has to\n3243|leave those tables deliberately. Five further gaps the disclosure surfaced:\n3244|Section 15.2's code-to-exit registry is implemented in\n3245|[`internal/axerror`](internal/axerror) and checked row by row, and\n3246|[`internal/cliresult`](internal/cliresult) now maps a failure to that exact\n3247|process status through `Emit` and `ExitStatus`, but nothing in the tree calls\n3248|`os.Exit` with it - the only `os.Exit` calls are the `exit(1)` failure paths of\n3249|`cataloggen` and `tracecheck` - so clause `14.2#6` stays undischarged until an\n3250|`ax` binary exists; Section 7.3's closed Provider Manifest exists only as a catalog\n3251|row naming its URN; Section 6.5 requires the `required_capabilities` default to\n3252|be the platform lane minimum while `internal/config/validation.go` accepts only\n3253|an empty default; Section 17.2's single clause is an unknown-event reader\n3254|obligation while its binding now names the CLI Result machine reader `Read`,\n3255|which classifies one completed invocation rather than retaining any event, so it\n3256|does not discharge that clause either - an unknown error code and an inert\n3257|unknown extension are not an unknown session event, and the four numbered reader\n3258|rules that binding does implement carry no RFC 2119 keyword and are invisible to\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
